From 44328a396f15a858d7cc5032032a1c0dc07ffab3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 00:00:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 090/112] Filters: a ramp that moves far goes through real
 configurations, a third of an octave at a time

A filter's coefficients are nonlinear in its cutoff and resonance, so the straight per-sample line from the last
block's set to this one's passed through sets that belong to no configuration: 30 Hz -> 18 kHz in one block strayed
4 dB from the real ladder at 13 % resonance, and near self-oscillation mid-ramp sets sang where neither end does.
The ramp is now cut into pieces (Filter::rampKnots()): at the knots the filter's own setConfig() gives the
coefficients of a real configuration (cutoff in log frequency, resonance and morph in equal steps; one piece per
third of an octave, 1/16 of the resonance's range, 1/8 of morph's, at most 32 per block), in between the straight
line as before. Where the ramp crosses the cutoff at which the 12 / 24 dB ladders' extra feedback changes
(tannedFrequency 304587486, ~11.8 kHz), it jumps there between the two real configurations (a knot with two sets).
The drive ladder's knots keep the block's oversampling. The usual small change (envelope, LFO, knob) stays one piece
and costs nothing more; a configuration that doesn't change renders bit-exactly as before (filter_neutral static
hash unchanged against 80b6060c).

tests/filters lpf_ramp_math: every mid-ramp set within 0.39 dB of the real ladder where it is linear (was 4.4 dB),
none unstable where the real ladder at its moveability is stable; filter_neutral jumps (28 Hz <-> 16 kHz every
block): peak at most +0.2 dB over the held filters.

